uff"},{"word":"scruff of the neck"},{"english":"of a horse","translation":"of a horse","word":"withers"}]},"napalm":{"id":"46a896b8-26e8-4937-b4a4-5b021723f98f","word":"napalm","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"flammable substance","tags":["masculine"],"word":"napalm"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"A trio of needle-nosed silver Phantom jets descended from the clouds and dropped a colossal load of napalm on the jungle. Flames turned the screen red and orange. Silence followed.\n“You smell that?” Kilgore said, almost to himself. Then, louder, “You smell that?” Lance, the champion surfer, asked “What?” Kilgore, lit by the glow of burning trees, retorted, “Napalm, son—nothing else in the world smells like that. I love the smell of napalm in the morning. You know, one time we had a hill bombed for 12 hours, and when it was all over I walked up; we didn’t find one of ’em … not one stinking dink body. But the smell—you know, that gasoline smell—the whole hill—it smelled like … victory.”","ref":"2013 April 1, Robert M. Neer, “PARIAH”, in Napalm: An American Biography, Harvard University Press, →ISBN, →LCCN, page 140:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[100,106],[360,366],[436,442]],"id":"14fcb27c-cf27-40d1-afdc-56f7cccdc2fa"}],"attestations":[{"date":"from 1942","references":[]}],"links":[["highly","highly"],["flammable","flammable"],["viscous","viscous"],["substance","substance"],["stick","stick"],["burn","burn"],["warfare","warfare"],["incendiary","incendiary"],["wooded","wooded"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["A highly flammable, viscous substance, designed to stick to the body while burning, used in warfare as an incendiary especially in wooded areas."],"tags":["uncountable","usually"],"id":"8f933d4e-4c87-410a-b507-64c5b618f16a"},{"examples":[{"text":"When leaked, the controversial documents proved to be political napalm.","bold_text_offsets":[[64,70]],"type":"example","id":