orn on the head by Christian women (chiefly Roman Catholic and Eastern Orthodox) when entering a church, as a token of modesty."],"id":"d2c5507c-a71a-4d9c-89ae-004aace90622"},{"categories":["English ellipses","Philippine English"],"links":[["sanitary napkin","sanitary napkin#English"]],"raw_glosses":["(Philippines, dated in the US) Ellipsis of sanitary napkin."],"glosses":["Ellipsis of sanitary napkin."],"tags":["Philippines","abbreviation","alt-of","dated","ellipsis"],"alt_of":[{"word":"sanitary napkin"}],"raw_tags":["in the US"],"id":"b2c4e237-7f85-4bf9-95e5-db142dc60c52"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"naphtha":{"id":"c7d28733-fe4f-4aec-9c4e-f6f38c77c474","word":"naphtha","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"naturally occurring liquid petroleum","tags":["feminine"],"word":"nafta"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"liquid distilled from petroleum","tags":["feminine"],"word":"nafta"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"categories":["English dated terms"],"links":[["liquid","liquid"],["petroleum","petroleum"]],"raw_glosses":["(dated) Naturally occurring liquid petroleum."],"glosses":["Naturally occurring liquid petroleum."],"tags":["countable","dated","uncountable"],"id":"9b433832-9867-4714-b6bf-e71ffb3080d8"},{"examples":[{"text":"The turmoil went on—no rest, no peace. […] It was nearly eleven o'clock now, and he strolled out again. In the little fair created by the costers' barrows the evening only seemed beginning; and the naphtha flares made one's eyes ache, the men's voices grated harshly, and the girls' faces saddened one.","ref":"1918, W[illiam] B[abington] Maxwell, chapter VII, in The Mirror and the Lamp, Indianapolis, Ind.: The Bobbs-Merrill Company, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[198,205]],"id":"747b44ee-a2e8-4bd8-83af-e450cb0dc549"},{"text":"No, this is not a disentanglement from, but a progressive knotting into — they go in under archways, secret entrances of rotted concrete that only looked like loops of an underpass... certain trestles of blackened wood have moved slowly by overhead, and the smells begun of coal from days far to the past, smells of naphtha winters, of Sundays when no traffic came through...","ref":"1973, Thomas Pynchon, Gravity's Rainbow:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[316,323]],"id":"59953dbe-8817-4da5-b3fe-6640cd8f5ee7"},{"text":"The Common Room and the Library were lit by anbaric light, but the Scholars preferred the older, softer naphtha lamps in the Retiring Room.","ref":"1995, Philip Pullman, Northern Lights:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[104,111]],"id":"3fc53c29-7b18-4a21-b4ff-d362d93d1925"}],"topics":["chemistry","geography","geology","natural-sciences","petrochemistry","petrology","physical-sciences"],"categories":["English terms with quotations","Quotation templates to be cleaned","en:Petrochemistry"],"links":[["petrochemistry","petrochemistry"],["aliphatic","aliphatic"],["aromatic","aromatic"],["hydrocarbon","hydrocarbon"],["petroleum","petroleum"],["coal tar","coal tar"],["solvent","solvent"],["petrol","petrol"]],"raw_glosses":["(petrochemistry) Any of a wide variety of aliphatic or aromatic liquid hydrocarbon mixtures distilled from petroleum or coal tar, especially as used in solvents or petrol."],"glosses":["Any of a wide variety of aliphatic or aromatic liquid hydrocarbon mixtures distilled from petroleum or coal tar, especially as used in solvents or petrol."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"13d6fb63-566f-4cc9-8ea1-f25437396fe7"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"napier's bones":{"id":"3d20bab5-d2ef-4fac-8643-c5d53b92a36d","word":"Napier's bones","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"a set of numbered rods used for multiplication and division","tags":["plural"],"word":"ossos de Napier"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"topics":["arithmetic"],"categories":["English entries with incorrect language header","English eponyms","English lemmas","English multiword terms","English nouns","English pluralia tantum","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Greek translations","Terms with Hungarian translations","Terms with Irish translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Spanish translations","en:Arithmetic"],"links":[["arithmetic","arithmetic"]],"raw_glosses":["(arithmetic) A set of numbered rods used for multiplication and division (see the Wikipedia article for a fuller explanation)."],"glosses":["A set of numbered rods used for multiplication and division (see the Wikipedia article for a fuller explanation)."],"tags":["plural","plural-only"],"id":"4d102dc8-8934-49d4-9ccf-d635a2c7ab6b"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"napoleonic":{"id":"fdfc858f-d4b3-4c2b-afb4-26221640eb68","word":"Napoleonic","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"of or pertaining to Napoleon Bonaparte","tags":["Portugal"],"word":"napoleónico"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"of or pertaining to Napoleon Bonaparte","tags":["Brazil"],"word":"napoleônico"}],"pos":"adj","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"tags":["collocation"],"text":"Napoleonic Wars","bold_text_offsets":[[0,10]],"type":"example","id":"ddc95e50-1f7f-4bec-91e7-07315f89a227"},{"text":"A half-burned cigar rolled between his mobile lips, he sat on the back of his neck, and yet he had an air Napoleonic; Nietzschean, it might better be said—although it is safe to assert that these moulders of American institutions knew little about that terrible philosopher who had raised his voice against the “slave morals of Christianity.”","ref":"1917, Winston Churchill, The Dwelling-Place of Light, New York, N.Y.: The Macmillan Company, page 341:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[106,116]],"id":"5378c41b-f62c-